<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jayanegara</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. <a href="/years/1309">1309</a>–<a href="/years/1328">1328</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Jayanagara, Kala Gemet</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jayanegara was the second monarch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/majapahit-empire">Majapahit Empire</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a>, ruling from 1309 until his death in 1328, and the only son of its founder, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raden-wijaya">Raden Wijaya</a>. Accounts differ over his mother: inscriptions name Queen Tribhuwana, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pararaton">Pararaton</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagarakretagama">Nagarakretagama</a> name the Malayu princess Dara Petak. His reign was troubled by rebellions, above all that of Kuti in 1319, which seized the capital until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gajah-mada">Gajah Mada</a>, commander of the palace guard, crushed it and began his rise at court. The Pararaton calls him Kala Gemet, the weak villain. He was killed by his doctor Tanca in 1328, and the true nature of the murder remains unknown.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The throne passed within the house of Raden Wijaya, and a woman could hold it: when <b>Jayanegara</b> was murdered in 1328, childless, the Queen Grandmother Gayatri Rajapatni, who had become a Buddhist nun, set her daughter Tribhuwana on it.</blockquote><a href="/societies/majapahit">Social Hierarchy of Majapahit</a></li></ul></section>
